## Findings by Severity

### 🔴 HIGH SEVERITY

#### 1. Conditional Test Logic
**Academic name:** Conditional Test Logic (Peruma et al., ESEC/FSE 2020)
**Affected test:** `ProcessOrder_SetsCorrectStatus` (line 31)

```csharp
if (result.TotalAmount > 100)
    Assert.AreEqual("PremiumProcessed", result.Status);
else
    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
```

**Risk:** One branch never executes — the test silently hides a gap. If the threshold changes, the test still passes but verifies the wrong status.

**Fix — split into two explicit tests:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_StandardOrder_SetsStandardStatus()
{
    var processor = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory);
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-1", 1) } };

    var result = processor.ProcessOrder(order);

    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
}

[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_PremiumOrder_SetsPremiumStatus()
{
    var processor = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory);
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-PREMIUM", 10) } };

    var result = processor.ProcessOrder(order);

    Assert.AreEqual("PremiumProcessed", result.Status);
}
```

---

#### 2. Assertion-Free Test (a.k.a. Unknown Test)
**Academic name:** Unknown Test / Empty Test (Peruma et al., ESEC/FSE 2020)
**Affected test:** `ProcessOrder_CompletesWithoutError` (line 43)

```csharp
public void ProcessOrder_CompletesWithoutError()
{
    var processor = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory);
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-1", 1) } };
    processor.ProcessOrder(order);
    // ← no assertion
}
```

**Risk:** This test passes even if `ProcessOrder` returns garbage, corrupts state, or silently fails. It only catches unhandled exceptions — giving false confidence in coverage.

**Fix — assert on the expected outcome:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_ValidOrder_ReturnsProcessedResult()
{
    var processor = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory);
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-1", 1) } };

    var result = processor.ProcessOrder(order);

    Assert.IsNotNull(result);
    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
}
```

