### 🟡 MEDIUM SEVERITY

#### 5. Eager Test
**Academic name:** Eager Test (Peruma et al., ESEC/FSE 2020)
**Affected test:** `OrderProcessor_FullWorkflow_Succeeds` (line 52)

```csharp
processor.ValidateOrder(order);
processor.CalculateTotal(order);
processor.ApplyDiscount(order, "SAVE10");
processor.ReserveInventory(order);
processor.ProcessPayment(order, new CreditCard("4111111111111111"));
processor.SendConfirmation(order);
processor.UpdateOrderHistory(order);
Assert.AreEqual("Completed", order.Status);
```

**Risk:** 7 distinct production methods called. A failure could originate in any of them, making diagnosis difficult. The single assertion only checks the final status — intermediate corruption goes undetected.

**Fix — split into focused tests per behavior, or explicitly mark as integration:**
```csharp
[TestCategory("Integration")]
[TestMethod]
public void OrderProcessor_FullWorkflow_Succeeds() { /* ... */ }
```
Or better, write separate unit tests for `ApplyDiscount`, `ReserveInventory`, etc.

